From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 15:06:20 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-12-276] MEME: Watered Down Extropianism?

Vince Kerchner wrote:
>Simon D. Levy <levy@cs.utk.edu> sez:
>
>>History shows that radical philosophies inevitably get watered down as
>>they become more popular.  For example, compare the radical communitarianism
>>and poverty of the early Christians (as mandated by Jesus in the Gospels)
>>with what the Catholic Church rapidly became.

>  (1) Until very recently (historically speaking), very few people could
>      either read or write.  Therefore, all memes had to be spread by word
>      of mouth, and had to depend upon the memory and cooperation of the
>      human intermediate vector for stability and fecundity.  This is no
>      longer a restriction.

On the other hand, now memes have to compete much more strongly. Note 
that a meme is easier to remember if it has some shock value, but it will 
also be more prone to attack from other memes. A meme with little shock 
value or similar to common memes in the meme pool will encounter less
resistance, and can spread through normal channels. This is apparently 
why the media have become more bland as the technology have advanced.

Another issue is: do we want to spread watered down extropianism? It will 
be much easier to spread and get accepted, on the expense of "ideological 
purity".

>  (2) Across generations, loss of memetic integrity in oral systems may
>      largely be due to the death of the original meme carriers, and their
>      subsequent replacement by newcomers who do not accurately reflect the
>      original belief set.  Once we have eliminated death, and once we
>      are able to pass memory and experience to our offspring without the
>      need for serial education, this too will no longer be a restriction.

However, the problem with memetic dilution of the extropian meme is a 
concern today, not in the far future (where it probably flourishes if 
death is eliminated). 

