From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 10:37:13 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-279] Privatization of National Parks.

>As long as clear cutting, tree planting and managed cultivation
>go hand in hand there isn't much of a problem.
> 
>And let us not forget that areas can be revegetated.

Let us not forget that old-growth forests take hundreds of years to
develop, and you will not have any with this method.
It's not enough just to have "trees".  They're not all the same.
(This reminds me of a Doonesbury comic, where Trudeau had James Watt
say that concerns about endangered birds were silly because they
still had lots of pigeons.)

Also note that when preservation is by private individuals, each person
can at any time decide to "develop" whatever resource he's got.  For a
redwood forest to grow undisturbed for 500 years, you would need every
individual who ever owned that forest to decide every year for 500 years
that it was more advantageous to keep it uncut.  Just one swing in the
market in 500 years that makes cutting profitable is all it takes to
undercut hundreds of years of conservation.

