From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 15:07:33 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-285] Privatization of National Parks.

>>Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu
>>Also note that when preservation is by private individuals, each person
>>can at any time decide to "develop" whatever resource he's got.  For a
>>redwood forest to grow undisturbed for 500 years, you would need every
>>individual who ever owned that forest to decide every year for 500 years
>>that it was more advantageous to keep it uncut.  Just one swing in the
>>market in 500 years that makes cutting profitable is all it takes to
>>undercut hundreds of years of conservation.
>
>Lately, forestry people have recognized that the burning of forests is
>perfectly natural. It happened before man's intervention due to lightning
>strikes.  Also, native Americans deliberately set massive fires.  Not
>letting forests burn allows an abnormal buildup of combustible material and
>makes fires hard to avoid and more damaging.  Part of Yellowstone was
>deliberately allowed to burn because of this new philosophy. I suspect that
>if the forest you manage gets too old then some future `super greens' will
>torch it to bring it back into harmony with nature.  gfs

Yes, burning is natural.  BUT:
1.  Burning is only helpful to quick-growth species like spruce.
    In fact, some pines and shrubs are adapted to reproduce after
    burns, so their sap and wood actually evolved to burn well.
    So it is mostly young forests that burn.
2.  Fire is not as destructive to old-growth forests like redwood
    and white pine as clearcutting.  Big trees are hard to kill.

What you're saying above is that it's natural to destroy an entire
forest every so often.  It isn't.  This is witnessed to by the fact
that old-growth forests contain a large percentage of trees hundreds of
years old, that either were not burned or survived the fire.

Forests do not get "too old".  Have you ever seen an old-growth forest?
Probably not, since they are now *extremely* rare in the US, and (I think)
nonexistant in Britain.  They have little undergrowth to spread fire,
mostly just canopy cover.  They have reached a stable population,
so there is no buildup of combustible materials.

I doubt that you could "manage" an old forest (meaning cutting down parts
of it periodically), because old-growth trees and new-growth trees are
incompatible.  The youngsters would be in the shadows of the old giants,
and do poorly; if some did well, they would increase the likelihood of
fire and endanger the old trees.

Phil goetz@cs.buffalo.edu

